{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And his master shall bring him to the door.</b> I might think whether it is uprooted or not uprooted — Scripture says מזוזה; just as the מזוזה is standing, so too the door is standing." "<b>And his master shall bore.</b> And not his son, and not his agent, and not an agent of the court. <b>His ear.</b> And not her ear, for there is no boring for a woman. <b>With an awl.</b> And it did not say במרצע (with a shuruk), which would imply a distinguished one among awls." "<b>And he shall serve him.</b> To him, and not to his son — from here we learn that he acquires himself at the death of the master. <b>Forever.</b> For the world of the Jubilee — thus did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound in tractate Qiddushin." "<b>And indeed.</b> It is well known in the world's custom, by the nature of the slave who stands under his master's authority, that he despises his life and is weary of living because of his labor, and he longs to be a free man. This one, then, who desires to remain in his slavery and to have upon himself a master other than the Holy One, blessed be He — this is a very extraordinary matter, and therefore the law concerning this is that the master himself, whom the slave has chosen and whom he has loved, shall bring him before the court, and shall bore him through at the door. This is so that the limb that sinned shall be punished — the ear that heard at Sinai 'You shall not steal' and he went and stole; or because it is written (Leviticus 25:55) ״כי לי בני ישראל עבדים״ — for to Me the children of Israel are slaves — and not slaves to slaves, and he went and acquired a master for himself. In any case, whichever it may be, this is counted to him as a sin, for he disowns the supernal Master, blessed be He, who warned him at the receiving of the Torah against servitude to any but Him, and he now casts off from himself the yoke of His commandment." "<b>And in my opinion.</b> Because of this it did not say 'When you buy a Hebrew slave' (כי תקנה עבד עברי), and it should have said so, or it should say כי תקנה ישראל לעבד — 'when you buy an Israelite as a slave' — for ישראל is the honorable name that was established upon us after the giving of the Torah; and behold, this one has come against what he heard at the giving of the Torah, and he is not worthy to be called by the name ישראל, so Scripture called him by the name עברי — this is the name of kinship, the name that Israel had while they were in Egypt without Torah, as it says, 'the Elohim of the Hebrews.' Therefore the Torah commanded that the limb of the sinner who heard the commandment and transgressed it be punished, and that it be punished by means of the master whom he chose, so that it be clarified from this that he and his master — both of them together, slave and master alike — are admonished regarding the service of the supernal Master, blessed be He, to whom all dominion and lordship belongs, not to flesh and blood. And this is what it says: והגישו אדניו אל האלהים — 'his master shall bring him to the Elohim' — this is the place of the court, which is the place of judgment, for judgment belongs to Elohim. And it said this corresponding to how the attribute of Judgment disowned lordship, which was the plague of the firstborn of Egypt, to deliver Israel from slavery. And it returned and further specified: והגישו אל הדלת או אל המזוזה — 'and he shall bring him to the door or to the doorpost' — corresponding to how the sign of the blood upon the doorways, upon the lintel and upon the two doorposts, through which Israel went out from slavery to freedom, was disowned. Therefore: ורצע אדניו את אזנו — 'his master shall bore his ear' — and blood issues from it, and all of this is measure for measure. And even though the drawing of blood from the ear is not a prerequisite, for if he pierces his ear with a substance no blood comes out, nevertheless the majority of those bored do not have such a substance, and blood does issue from them. And the name of the implement is called מרצע, and its gematria is ת (400), for it corresponds to what he wished to add upon the 400 years of slavery of Egypt." "<b>And they said</b> in the Midrash: ״והגישו אל הדלת״ — ״and you shall bring him to the door.״ The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I opened for him the door of the house to go out free, and he closed upon himself the door of the house to become a slave; therefore he shall be struck at the door." "<b>And you should understand further</b> that there is a secret in the word הדלת (the door), and therefore the word is mentioned with the definite article — ״והגישו אל הדלת״ — and likewise we find in Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 15:17) ״ונתתה באזנו ובדלת.״ This is because the door is the place of entry to the house, for it is impossible to enter the house except through the door, and the word דלת (door) is from the language of the letter דל״ת of the verse of שמע, which is Kenneset Yisra'el, and it is השער לה' אשר צדיקים יבאו בו (Psalms 118:20) — the gate of HaShem through which the righteous enter. And what it says, ״אל המזוזה״ (unto the doorpost) — it is a number, i.e., מזוזה in gematria is יהוה (the name of twenty-six; note: in some editions the printed text reads אדני), and this is clear. And this Hebrew slave neither knew nor understood this, and he exchanged holy lordship for profane lordship; therefore the commandment came that he shall serve his master until the time in which all rulers and lords, in the upper realms and in the lower, shall kneel before HaShem of lords, and all shall recognize that לה' הארץ ומלואה (Psalms 24:1) — the earth is HaShem's and its fullness." "<b>And he shall serve him לעולם (forever).</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received that this is the עולם (world/epoch) of the Jubilee, which is the fiftieth year, for a span of fifty years is called עולם; thus we find with Samuel the Ramathite (1 Samuel 1:22) ״ושב שם עד עולם״ — ״and he shall remain there forever״ — and that is the עולמו of Levi, as it says (Numbers 8:24) ״מבן חמש ועשרים שנה ועד בן חמשים שנה,״ and all the days of Samuel were no more than fifty-two years, and he had two years remaining at that time. And this law of the Jubilee applies whether the time be near or far. Alternatively, the sense of לעולם may be כל ימי עולם — all the days of the world — for since he serves until the Jubilee year, behold he is a slave all the days of the world; and it comes to hint that all the days of the world are the great Jubilee, and this is the matter of the verse (Psalms 105:8) ״דבר צוה לאלף דור״ — for a generation of fifty years is likewise like an עולם, and so Solomon, peace be upon him, hinted (Ecclesiastes 1:4) ״והארץ לעולם עומדת״ — that is the עולמו of the Jubilee, the עולם. And understand this. And in the portion of Behar Sinai I shall mention this when I arrive there, with the help of the Blessed Name."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {1 "The note הב' בשו\"א indicates the vowel point difference: the text has במרצע with a pataḥ under the bet, but with a shuruk it would mean 'a specific/unique awl'; the comment explains why the verse uses this form."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:36.157971443Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}